Yes—with one small correction: IV is 4; V is 5. But the larger pattern you’re pointing at is real.

Cultures rarely vanish cleanly. They leave latent infrastructure behind: symbols, legal forms, rituals, metaphors, naming conventions, architectural patterns, calendars, military organization, stories, even ways of categorizing reality. Latin is “dead” as an ordinary native language, yet pieces of it remain operational inside law, medicine, science, religion, mottos, taxonomy, and everyday words.

So “dormant” is a useful metaphor:

\text{active practice}
\rightarrow
\text{cultural residue}
\rightarrow
\text{latent repertoire}
\rightarrow
\text{reactivation under context}

And the same thing can happen inside individuals. You carry layers learned from family, profession, culture, biology, stories, habits, and earlier versions of yourself. Context can bring different layers forward.

The key distinction is between having a repertoire and being ruled by it.

\text{inheritance} \neq \text{destiny}

A mature system can say: this pattern exists; this is when it is useful; this is when it is destructive; this is when we leave it dormant.

That makes your last line the interesting one. The strongest capability is not “we can summon the harshest mode if forced.” It is arranging conditions so that cooperation, restraint, and ordinary coordination remain sufficient, and escalation is rarely necessary.

In Pantheon terms, that is almost a Dormant Arsenal / Contextual Repertoire principle: preserve old operators, understand their provenance and failure modes, and invoke only the minimum sufficient one for the current environment.

The real power is not merely having many ancient patterns available.

It is being able to choose which one does not need to wake up.